window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"][20]={"university_electrical_engineering_en:M20-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M20-L1 – From the Medical Question to the Measurand<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Biomedical Measurement Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish the physiological\ntarget quantity, the quantity that can actually be measured and the\ntechnical measurement requirement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement Task and Physiological Origin<\/h2>\n<p>A medical question is not yet an electrical measurement task. For\nexample, to study the temporal sequence of cardiac activity, an\nelectrocardiogram (ECG) can record electrical potential differences on\nthe body surface. An optical pulse sensor, by contrast, records changes\nin local tissue perfusion. Both signals may be periodic, but they\nmeasure different processes with different delays. A reading from a\nsingle channel neither represents the entire organ directly nor\nconstitutes a diagnosis on its own.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Target quantity or process<\/th>\n<th>Measurand<\/th>\n<th>Possible interference<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Electrical cardiac activity<\/td>\n<td>Potential difference (mV)<\/td>\n<td>Electrode movement, muscle activity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Peripheral pulse wave<\/td>\n<td>Modulation of optical power<\/td>\n<td>Movement, ambient light<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pressure<\/td>\n<td>Force per unit area (Pa)<\/td>\n<td>Zero drift, height difference<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Movement<\/td>\n<td>Acceleration (m/s²)<\/td>\n<td>Orientation, gravity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement Chain and Identifiability<\/h2>\n<p>A simplified linear model is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=g\\,(h*x)(t)+b+n(t)+a(t).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the target measurand,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe sensor impulse response,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe scaling factor,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe offset,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrandom noise and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na non-stationary artefact. The convolution describes the temporal\ndynamics. With a slow sensor, a rapid change in the target quantity may\nappear attenuated. Movement may even make\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntime-dependent; the purely additive interference model is then\ninsufficient.<\/p>\n<p>The inference from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nback to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis only unambiguous if the mapping preserves sufficient information. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(f)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a zero at a particular frequency, that component cannot be recovered\nby linear inversion. Two physiological processes that produce the same\nobservable pattern may also be indistinguishable without additional\ninformation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-and-information-technology-en/media/img/U_EIT_077_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Measurement chain with sensor, amplification, digitisation and processing, together with superimposed interference.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Sensor, analog conditioning and ADC convert a\nphysiological model quantity into digital measurements; a superimposed\ninterference signal shows why the entire chain matters.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Requirements and Uncertainty<\/h2>\n<p>The measurement requirement is described by the measurement range,\nbandwidth, temporal resolution, permissible errors and operating\nconditions. To record a signal with relevant components up to 35 Hz, the\nsampling rate must exceed 70 Hz; a real analogue low-pass filter also\nneeds a transition band. Choosing 250 Hz is one possible teaching\nexample, not a universally applicable medical standard. ADC sampling\nevery 4 ms limits temporal resolution when simply searching for maxima;\ninterpolation can provide sub-sample estimates but cannot reconstruct\nlost data.<\/p>\n<p>Independent standard uncertainties are approximately combined in\nquadrature. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(y-b)/g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nassuming independent input quantities,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_x^2\\approx\\frac{u_y^2+u_b^2}{g^2}+\\frac{(y-b)^2}{g^4}u_g^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Unknown systematic artefacts are not automatically covered by this\nuncertainty. A quality flag or rejection of a segment may therefore be\ntechnically preferable to an apparently precise numerical result.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Limits of the Exercises<\/h2>\n<p>A synthetic sensor delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.84<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=0.84<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV with an offset of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and sensitivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=0.40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV/mV. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.012<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_y=0.012<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.008<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_b=0.008<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_g=0.010<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV/mV, the result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.062<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_x\\approx0.062<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV. The result describes the assumed calibration model; an undetected\nmotion artefact can cause a substantially larger error.<\/p>\n<p>All exercises in this module use synthetic signals or data from\nsuitable published archives. Do not connect any self-built circuit to a\nperson. Patient protection, electrical isolation, risk management and\nclinical validation are separate tasks that the teaching model does not\ncover. Even battery-powered devices may have additional current paths\nthrough data cables.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What distinguishes ECG and PPG? <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\nElectrical potential difference versus optically detected volume change;\ntheir timing markers are not identical.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A channel has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV/mV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.85<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=0.85<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 1.5 mV.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two independent error contributions have standard uncertainties of 3\nand 4 units. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 5 units, not 7; independence is\nessential.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a stronger amplifier not recover a feature outside the\nsensor bandwidth? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> The feature is missing or\nhas already been attenuated too strongly relative to interference.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://you.stonybrook.edu/milab/courses/bme-313-bioinstrumentation/\">Stony\nBrook University – BME 313: Bioinstrumentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/hst-582j-biomedical-signal-and-image-processing-spring-2007/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Biomedical Signal and Image Processing<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2021.808451\">Park et al. –\nPhotoplethysmogram Analysis and Applications: An Integrative Review\n(2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M20-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M20-L2 – Bioelectrical Signals and Electrode Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Biomedical Measurement Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate electrode impedance,\ncommon-mode rejection and errors caused by unequal contacts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Bioelectrical Potentials<\/h2>\n<p>Ion transport across cell membranes is one cause of time-dependent\nelectrical potentials. An ECG does not measure the voltage within an\nindividual cell, but the potential differences between electrodes\narising from distributed bioelectrical sources. Position, orientation,\nspatial conductivity and lead configuration affect the measured\nwaveform. The P wave, QRS complex and T wave in a surface ECG provide a\ntemporal description of electrical cardiac activity; mechanical activity\nfollows with dynamics of its own.<\/p>\n<p>The synthetic signal used in M20-L5 combines individual bell-shaped\npulses. It imitates only selected waveform features, not the physics of\ncardiac muscle or a clinical lead system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Electrical Equivalent Circuit<\/h2>\n<p>In the small-signal regime, the electrode–electrolyte interface can\nbe described by an offset source\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an impedance consisting of a series resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a parallel resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand capacitance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_e(j\\omega)=R_s+\\frac{R_p}{1+j\\omega R_p C_p}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This teaching model accounts neither for all electrochemical\nprocesses nor for the nonlinearity and temporal variation of real\ncontacts. At DC, the impedance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s+R_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat very high frequencies, it approaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkΩ,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_p=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkΩ and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_p=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnF, the time constant is 10 ms and the characteristic frequency is 15.9\nHz. The voltage at the amplifier input also depends on its input\nimpedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_i=V_q Z_i/(Z_i+Z_e)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Differential Measurement and Common Mode<\/h2>\n<p>For two input potentials,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_d=v_+-v_-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c=(v_++v_-)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith differential gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand common-mode gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit follows that<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_o=A_d v_d+A_c v_c,\\qquad \\mathrm{CMRR}=20\\log_{10}|A_d/A_c|.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At 100 dB CMRR and a common-mode voltage of 0.2 V, the equivalent\ninput-referred differential error is 2 µV. This applies to the amplifier\nalone at the frequency under consideration. Unequal contact impedances\nconvert part of the common-mode signal into a differential voltage even\nbefore subtraction. In the purely resistive model with two equal input\nimpedances\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand contact resistances\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1,R_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{d,c}=v_c\\left(\\frac{R_i}{R_i+R_1}-\\frac{R_i}{R_i+R_2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-and-information-technology-en/media/img/U_EIT_078_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Magnitude of a complex electrode impedance and modelled common-mode conversion caused by unequal contact resistances.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Complex electrode contact impedance depends on\nfrequency; unequal contact resistances can convert common-mode\ncomponents into interfering differential signals.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Artefacts<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nMΩ,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkΩ,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_2=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkΩ and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV, the result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.795<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{d,c}\\approx0.795<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV. The error can therefore be of the same order of magnitude as an\neducational ECG signal, even though the amplifier itself has a high\nCMRR. The simple calculation assumes an ideal common source relative to\nthe modelled reference. Real capacitive coupling, return paths, input\nfilters and protection circuits alter the result.<\/p>\n<p>Contact movement can change the interface potentials and impedances.\nMuscle activity is another electrical source, not merely electronic\nnoise. Both types of interference may share spectral components with the\ndesired signal. Separation cannot then be achieved by a cutoff frequency\nalone. PhysioNet documents empirically recorded interference signals for\nthis purpose; it is cited here as a further resource, not as the data\nused in the simulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat zero frequency in the example above. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 102\nkΩ.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the effect of a very large\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Reduced loading of the source; other sources\nof error remain possible.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the input-referred common-mode error for 80 dB and 0.1 V.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 10 µV.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Set both contact resistances equal. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> The\ndifferential error caused by common-mode conversion in the\nvoltage-divider model becomes zero; the finite CMRR remains.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://you.stonybrook.edu/milab/courses/bme-313-bioinstrumentation/\">Stony\nBrook University – BME 313: Bioinstrumentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/hst-582j-biomedical-signal-and-image-processing-spring-2007/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Biomedical Signal and Image Processing<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/nstdb/1.0.0/\">PhysioNet –\nMIT-BIH Noise Stress Test Database v1.0.0<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M20-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M20-L3 – Optical and Mechanical Measurement Principles<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Biomedical Measurement Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret optical pulse\nmodulation and mechanical sensor characteristics quantitatively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Photoplethysmography<\/h2>\n<p>In photoplethysmography (PPG), a light source illuminates tissue and\na photodiode measures transmitted or backscattered light. The signal\ncontains a slowly varying background (DC) and a pulsatile component\n(AC). The sign of the latter depends, among other things, on the optical\narrangement and electrical signal definition. Pronounced movement,\nchanges in contact force and ambient light may affect the measured\nsignal.<\/p>\n<p>For an idealised homogeneous medium,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=P_0\\exp(-\\mu l)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napplies. For small changes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the effective path length,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>P<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\Delta P}{P}\\approx-\\mu\\Delta l.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In scattering tissue,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a simple geometric thickness but, at best, an effective model\nparameter. An example with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncm<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi><\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta l=0{.}01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncm yields a relative change of approximately -1%. A high background\noptical power therefore does not automatically imply high pulsatile\nsignal quality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two Wavelengths and Pulse Features<\/h2>\n<p>Pulse oximetry exploits the wavelength-dependent absorption\nproperties of oxygenated and deoxygenated haemoglobin. A widely used\nintermediate quantity is the ratio of the normalised pulsatile\ncomponents at two wavelengths:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\frac{AC_{rot}/DC_{rot}}{AC_{infrarot}/DC_{infrarot}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The original subscripts rot and infrarot denote red and infrared,\nrespectively. Converting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto a saturation value requires suitable device-specific calibration.\nThis conversion cannot be performed using an arbitrary single PPG\nchannel. If the normalised components are 0.006 and 0.010, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0{.}6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ndeliberately, no saturation value is derived here. The time between\npulse maxima also provides an estimate of the pulse period. Due to\nmechanical delay and pulse propagation, ECG and PPG have different time\nmarkers; changes in the time offset cannot be uniquely attributed to\nblood pressure without further modelling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Pressure and Motion<\/h2>\n<p>A pressure sensor converts force per unit area into an electrical\nquantity. For an ideally linearly calibrated measurement bridge,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=g_p p+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay serve as a model. Pressure is a local scalar quantity. A liquid\ncolumn of constant density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces an additional hydrostatic pressure<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p=\\rho g\\Delta h.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>An accelerometer, by contrast, measures specific force along its\naxes, in a simplified model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒇<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒈<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf f=\\mathbf a-\\mathbf g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA stationary sensor therefore generally does not produce a zero signal.\nOrientation and dynamic acceleration cannot be uniquely separated from a\nsingle instantaneous acceleration value. Sensor fusion may help;\nfundamentals of coordinate systems and signal estimation are covered in\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L3<\/a> and\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-and-information-technology-en/media/img/U_EIT_079_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic optical pulse modulation and hydrostatic pressure error as a function of height.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Synthetic optical pulse modulation rides on a much\nlarger background-light component; a change in measurement height also\ncreates a hydrostatic pressure offset.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>For a liquid with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1060<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1060<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkg/m³ and a height difference of 20 cm, the pressure difference is\napproximately 2080 Pa or 15.6 mmHg (1 mmHg corresponds to approximately\n133.322 Pa). The sign depends on which measurement point is higher. This\ncalculation illustrates the order of magnitude of a hydrostatic effect;\nit simulates neither vascular circulation nor a specific blood pressure\ninstrument.<\/p>\n<p>If the mean photodiode current is 50 µA and the pulsatile peak\namplitude is 0.5 µA, the normalised AC/DC ratio is 1%. A change in\nambient light that introduces 1 µA within the signal band can exceed the\npulsatile component. Shielding, time-controlled illumination and quality\nindicators address different causes; a low-pass filter alone does not\nsolve this problem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The pulse maxima are 0.75 s apart. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 80\npulses/min as a periodic model estimate.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The two AC/DC values are 0.008 and 0.016. <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0{.}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nno device-independent saturation value can be derived.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What pressure difference results from a height difference of 10 cm\nand a density of 1000 kg/m³? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 981 Pa or\napproximately 7.36 mmHg.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can an accelerometer alone reliably remove all motion artefacts from\na PPG signal? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> No; local mechanical and\noptical changes may be independent or nonlinearly coupled.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2021.808451\">Park et al. –\nPhotoplethysmogram Analysis and Applications: An Integrative Review\n(2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://you.stonybrook.edu/milab/courses/bme-313-bioinstrumentation/\">Stony\nBrook University – BME 313: Bioinstrumentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M20-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M20-L4 – Measurement Amplification, Filtering and Feature Extraction<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Biomedical Measurement Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Size amplification and\nquantisation appropriately, and assess the benefits and distortion\nintroduced by filters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dynamic Range and Gain<\/h2>\n<p>A measurement chain must resolve small desired signals without\nclipping due to offsets or interference. For an ideal bipolar ADC with a\nrange of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>±<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits, the step size is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>q<\/mi><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=\\frac{2U}{2^N},\\qquad q_\\mathrm{in}=\\frac{q}{A_d}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>65<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=1{.}65<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_d=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe step size is 0.806 mV at the ADC and approximately 4.03 µV referred\nto the input. A 1 mV pulse produces 0.2 V at the ADC. An additional 10\nmV offset, however, would already produce 2 V at the same gain and cause\nclipping. The position of the offset correction and the available\nheadroom are therefore important. An ideally uniformly distributed\nquantisation error has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>12<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_q=q/\\sqrt{12}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthis assumption does not apply without qualification to slow or\ndeterministic signals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Filters and Temporal Resolution<\/h2>\n<p>An analogue anti-aliasing filter must act before sampling. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>250<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=250<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz, an insufficiently attenuated 230 Hz component appears as 20 Hz after\nsampling. A digital filter can no longer distinguish it by its original\nfrequency. High-pass, low-pass and band-stop filters address different\ninterference components. For a simplified RC low-pass filter,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|H(j2\\pi f)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(f/f_c)^2}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=f_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe magnitude decreases to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA causal filter also changes the phase; frequency-dependent group delay\naffects time markers. Offline, filtering may be performed in both\nforward and backward directions. With real filter coefficients, this\nyields an effective magnitude of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|H|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand zero phase for an idealised infinitely long signal. Transients occur\nat the boundaries; the method requires future samples and cannot be used\nas an immediate real-time filter.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-and-information-technology-en/media/img/U_EIT_080_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Frequency response of a teaching filter and changes in a synthetic ECG segment caused by filtering.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The frequency response of a model filter changes the\nshape of a synthetic ECG excerpt; stronger attenuation of some\nfrequencies can alter diagnostically relevant waveform\ncomponents.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Features, Quality Indicators and Evaluation<\/h2>\n<p>A simple peak detection method combines a minimum height, prominence\nand minimum separation. Prominence describes the elevation above the\nsurrounding signal level. Minimum separation prevents multiple\ndetections within a short time but may also suppress genuine events that\nare close together. Two detected time markers yield the instantaneous\nevent rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=60/(t_2-t_1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\nmin<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi><\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nif time is measured in seconds.<\/p>\n<p>A measurable evaluation requires independently annotated reference\nevents and a specified tolerance. With one-to-one matching, correctly\ndetected events are true positives (TP), incorrectly detected events are\nfalse positives (FP), and missed events are false negatives (FN). The\nfollowing applies:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Sensitivity}=\\frac{TP}{TP+FN},\\qquad \\mathrm{Precision}=\\frac{TP}{TP+FP}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Both quantities are undefined if their denominators are zero. High\nsensitivity can coexist with many falsely detected pulses. Timing\nerrors, amplitude distortion, the proportion of rejected signal segments\nand the conditions under which reference data are collected also matter.\nMetrics for synthetic pulses must not be generalised as evidence of\nclinical performance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example<\/h2>\n<p>With 24 reference pulses and 29 detections, 24 of which are correctly\nmatched, sensitivity is 100%, but precision is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>29<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>82<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24/29\\approx82{.}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>%.\nA filter can reduce the number of false detections while changing the\npulse shape. A narrower bandwidth is therefore an improvement only if it\ndoes not distort the target quantities beyond acceptable limits. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L5<\/a>, these\neffects are measured separately using known reference signals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What input-referred quantisation step results from doubling the gain\nto 400? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> Approximately 2.01 µV; the\nundistorted input range is halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What alias frequency results from a 260 Hz component at a sampling\nrate of 250 Hz? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 10 Hz.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">There are 20 reference events, 18 true positives and 3 false\npositives. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> Sensitivity 90%, precision\n85.7%.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can the forward-backward filtered waveform not directly\nrepresent a real-time detector? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> It uses\nfuture signal samples.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/hst-582j-biomedical-signal-and-image-processing-spring-2007/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Biomedical Signal and Image Processing<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/nstdb/1.0.0/\">PhysioNet –\nMIT-BIH Noise Stress Test Database v1.0.0<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://you.stonybrook.edu/milab/courses/bme-313-bioinstrumentation/\">Stony\nBrook University – BME 313: Bioinstrumentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M20-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M20-L5 – Case Study: Biosignal Measurement Chain<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Biomedical Measurement Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L1<\/a> to <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate a synthetic biosignal\nchain reproducibly and distinguish signal quality from detection\nquality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Experimental Setup and Reference<\/h2>\n<p>A 20 s ECG-like test signal sampled at 250 Hz (5000 samples) is\ninvestigated. It contains 24 known reference maxima separated by 0.8 s,\ncorresponding to a constant model rate of 75/min. Idealised Gaussian\npulses are added around each time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/munder><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(t)=\\sum_k\\sum_i A_i\\exp\\left[-\\frac{(t-r_k-\\delta_i)^2}{2\\sigma_i^2}\\right].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(mV)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(s)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>P-like<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.170<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.035<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q-like<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.15<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.035<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.012<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R-like<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.00<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.016<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S-like<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.035<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.014<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T-like<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.28<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.240<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.075<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The reference markers are the constructed centres of the R-like\npulses, not clinically annotated cardiac events. The model waveforms,\ninterference amplitudes and thresholds were selected for demonstration\npurposes. Three signal variants are compared: the reference; the\nreference with drift (0.27 Hz, 0.35 mV), 50 Hz interference (0.16 mV)\nand white noise (standard deviation 0.045 mV); and the same mixture with\nthree additional local motion artefact pulses. The same noise\nrealisation is used in both disturbed cases to reveal the effect of the\nadditional interference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible Evaluation<\/h2>\n<p>Each variant is evaluated both unfiltered and using a digital\nButterworth band-pass filter (prototype order 3, 0.5–35 Hz,\nforward-backward processing). A height of 0.45 mV, prominence of 0.35\nmV, minimum separation of 0.3 s and matching tolerance of 60 ms are\nidentical for all cases. Each detection may be matched to only one\nreference event. Because the tolerance windows are disjoint, the\nmatching method used is unambiguous with respect to the number of true\npositives. The root-mean-square error (RMSE) measures deviation from the\nunfiltered reference signal, while timing RMSE considers only matched\npulses. A filter may therefore affect these two quantities\ndifferently.<\/p>\n<p>The following Python programme requires NumPy and SciPy and uses a\nfixed random seed. All signal values are modelled in mV; no measurement\nfiles are read. The original output labels Referenz, Drift + Netz +\nRauschen and Zusätzlich Bewegung correspond to Reference, Drift + mains\ninterference + noise and Additional motion. False and True indicate\nunfiltered and filtered processing, respectively.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.signal <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> butter, sosfiltfilt, find_peaks<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>fs<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">250<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>fs)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> truth<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"fl\">0.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">19.6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.8<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> gauss(c,w): <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>((t<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>c)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>w)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">.12<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>gauss(r<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.17<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.035<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.15<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>gauss(r<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.035<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.012<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>gauss(r,<span class=\"fl\">.016<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>gauss(r<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.035<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.014<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.28<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>gauss(r<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.24<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.075<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> r <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> truth)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">2026<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> noise<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.045<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>rng.standard_normal(t.size)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>base<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.35<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sin(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.27<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>t)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> mains<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.16<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sin(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">50<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>t)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>motion<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.1<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>gauss(<span class=\"fl\">8.35<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.045<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.4<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>gauss(<span class=\"fl\">12.35<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.025<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.7<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>gauss(<span class=\"fl\">12.48<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.07<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>sos<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>butter(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,[<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">35<\/span>],btype<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;bandpass&#39;<\/span>,fs<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>fs,output<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;sos&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> evaluate(x,filtered):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>sosfiltfilt(sos,x) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> filtered <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> x<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    p,_<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>find_peaks(y,height<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.45<\/span>,distance<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>fs),prominence<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.35<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    unmatched<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>fs)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> errors<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> r <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> truth:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> unmatched:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            k<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>(np.argmin(np.<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(np.array(unmatched)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>r)))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(unmatched[k]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>r)<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.06<\/span>: errors.append(unmatched.pop(k)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>r)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    tp<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(errors)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> fp<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(unmatched)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> fn<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(truth)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>tp<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    rmse<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.sqrt(np.mean((y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> timing<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.sqrt(np.mean(np.square(errors)))<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> errors <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;nan&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> tp<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>fn<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(truth) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> tp<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>fp<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(p)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> y,p,tp,fp,fn,rmse,timing<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cases<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>{<span class=\"st\">&#39;Referenz&#39;<\/span>:s,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Drift + Netz + Rauschen&#39;<\/span>:s<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>base<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>mains<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>noise,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Zusätzlich Bewegung&#39;<\/span>:s<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>base<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>mains<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>noise<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>motion}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">__name__<\/span><span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;__main__&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> name,x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> cases.items():<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> f <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            y,p,tp,fp,fn,rmse,timing<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>evaluate(x,f)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(name,f,tp,fp,fn,<span class=\"ss\">f&#39;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>rmse<span class=\"sc\">:.4f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"ss\">f&#39;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>timing<span class=\"sc\">:.2f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">&#39;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Results<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario<\/th>\n<th>Filter<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">TP<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">FP<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">FN<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">RMSE (mV)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Timing RMSE (ms)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference<\/td>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.1119<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drift + mains interference + noise<\/td>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.2774<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.92<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drift + mains interference + noise<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.1157<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Additional motion<\/td>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.3031<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.92<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Additional motion<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.1529<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-and-information-technology-en/media/img/U_EIT_081_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic signal with a motion artefact and number of falsely detected pulses before and after filtering.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A movement artefact can remain as an apparent peak\neven after filtering; the detection comparison shows why a smooth trace\ndoes not prove error-free pulse detection.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Filtering reduces low-frequency baseline variation and\nhigher-frequency interference. In the motion case, however, two falsely\ndetected events remain: precision increases from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>31<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>77<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24/31\\approx77{.}4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>%\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>26<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>92<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24/26\\approx92{.}3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>%,\nnot to 100%. Even the clean signal has an RMSE of 0.1119 mV after\nfiltering. In the clean case the detection time markers are preserved on\nthe sampling grid, but the waveform changes. A timing RMSE of zero means\nonly that the discrete time markers coincide within the constructed\nreference used here, not that temporal accuracy is infinite.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validation and Transferability<\/h2>\n<p>The exactly known model events allow the matching procedure and error\ncounts to be checked. In every case,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">TP+FN=24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust hold; furthermore,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">TP+FP<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals the number of detections. This is a test of the evaluation logic,\nnot an independent validation of real measurements. The simulated\nperiodic waveform and relatively low variability favour detection.\nIsolated artefacts outside the reference windows are easier to assess\nhere than interference overlapping the desired pulses.<\/p>\n<p>A useful extension would vary pulse intervals, widths and amplitudes,\nincrease drift or motion and use several independent random sequences.\nSuitably annotated public ECG datasets and the MIT-BIH Noise Stress Test\nDatabase may be used to evaluate performance on real data. Detector\nparameters must not be optimised on the same test material and then\ndescribed as independently validated. A real-time implementation must\nadditionally document causality, latency and rejected segments. This\nlesson is not intended for clinical use or personal medical\nassessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the precision for the unfiltered case with drift, mains\ninterference and noise? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 24/29 = 82.76%.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the filtered reference exhibit a non-zero signal deviation?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> The filter also changes spectral components\nof the desired signal, even though no noise is present.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the sampling interval? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 4 ms;\ntiming error is evaluated on this grid.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Increase the artefact amplitude in the code or shift an artefact\npulse onto a reference pulse. <strong>Solution guidance:<\/strong>\nPerformance then depends on the local superposition and detection\nthreshold. Compare TP, FP, FN and RMSE separately; a small timing RMSE\ndoes not rule out missed events.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does 100% sensitivity not constitute clinical evidence?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Only one uniform synthetic signal type and a\nfew interference variants were tested, not the diversity of real data\nand operating conditions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/hst-582j-biomedical-signal-and-image-processing-spring-2007/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Biomedical Signal and Image Processing<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/nstdb/1.0.0/\">PhysioNet –\nMIT-BIH Noise Stress Test Database v1.0.0<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
